  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">APEC Occupational Standards promoting skilled tourism workforces in APEC
&#x200d;
Australia, via the &#x2018;Sub-regional Pilot of the APEC Occupational Standards for the Travel, Tourism and Hospitality Industry in the Pacific Alliance&#x2019; project, led a collaborative endeavour with the Pacific Alliance economies of Peru, Chile, Colombia, and Mexico to test the applicability of the APEC Occupational Standards for the occupations of Cook and Housekeeper, mapping domestic standards against those developed through APEC. Through this process, which involved over 170 industry and 75 institutional representatives, the Pilot demonstrated that the APEC Occupational Standards were relevant to each of the local labour markets and could be used flexibly to inform domestic standards development, updates to training curriculum and the recognition of skills held by workers. These findings will provide valuable evidence for APEC members as they continue to pursue longer term objectives for the international certification of skills competencies and enhanced regional labour mobility.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023-24, the Australian-funded APEC Business Activator Pilot for Women-led SMEs to Access Global Markets engaged women entrepreneurs directly to strengthen their capacity to build business competencies and help to navigate barriers to expanding local and international trade. The pilot supported 19 entrepreneurs from Indonesia, Malaysia, Mexico, Papua New Guinea, Peru, Thailand, the Philippines, Chinese Taipei and Vietnam through 10 online workshops, ongoing group mentoring and a three-day intensive program in Melbourne, alongside International Women&#x2019;s Day events. Entrepreneurs were providing business development services, including training to identify international clients, prepare compelling market offerings, determine market entry strategies, secure funding, and develop financial game plans and robust business models. The project has helped to increase business networks, and supported international expansions and increased market presence for participants. This signature Australian initiative was co-designed with RMIT Activator, and is expected to continue in the next phase.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
